WEBVTT

01-01
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.724
Welcome to this review of Gibbs energy graphs and liquid stability.

01-02
00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:11.987
We will follow one calculation from the graph to the equations, then
reproduce it in Lab zero four.

01-03
00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:20.272
Our central question is simple: for a fixed overall composition, which
allowed state has the lowest Gibbs energy?

01-04
00:00:20.452 --> 00:00:31.137
By the end, you should be able to distinguish phase compositions from
phase amounts, explain a metastable liquid, and use the Lab to check
your own reasoning.

01-05
00:00:31.317 --> 00:00:33.579
Keep a calculator nearby.

01-06
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:38.751
You will have several opportunities to pause and work before seeing
the answer.

02-01
00:00:41.281 --> 00:00:46.806
Open the four-page calculation and Lab guide using the link on this
slide.

02-02
00:00:46.986 --> 00:00:50.570
Also open Lab zero four in another tab.

02-03
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.227
Start with sections A and B on pages one and two.

02-04
00:00:54.407 --> 00:01:01.113
Later, use section C for the screen demonstration and section D for
independent practice.

02-05
00:01:01.293 --> 00:01:05.923
Your calculator must have natural logarithms, labeled L N.

02-06
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:10.839
The suggested times on the slide describe the original reading route.

02-07
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:16.629
The video has its own running time, and your calculation pauses add to
that time.

02-08
00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:21.033
This is optional support for your original Week three worksheet.

02-09
00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:24.883
It adds no attendance or exit-ticket submission.

03-01
00:01:27.413 --> 00:01:30.388
First, separate consistency from stability.

03-02
00:01:30.568 --> 00:01:41.085
Lab zero three asks whether data are compatible with an applicable
thermodynamic constraint, such as Gibbs Duhem, under the assumptions
of the test.

03-03
00:01:41.265 --> 00:01:46.982
Lab zero four asks which feasible state has the lowest total Gibbs
energy.

03-04
00:01:47.162 --> 00:01:49.232
These are different questions.

03-05
00:01:49.412 --> 00:01:54.212
A consistent liquid model can predict separation into two liquids.

03-06
00:01:54.392 --> 00:01:59.789
In guide A one, identify which question a common tangent helps answer.

03-07
00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:07.100
Throughout this review, temperature and pressure are fixed, and we
consider only a binary liquid system.

03-08
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:12.486
Vapor and solid phases are outside the allowed states in this example.

04-01
00:02:15.016 --> 00:02:18.194
Read the axes before interpreting the shape.

04-02
00:02:18.374 --> 00:02:26.084
The horizontal coordinate, x, is the mole fraction of component one in
a possible homogeneous liquid.

04-03
00:02:26.264 --> 00:02:32.690
The vertical coordinate, g, is the molar Gibbs energy of mixing
divided by R T.

04-04
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.620
It is dimensionless.

04-05
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.002
The symbol z describes the overall feed composition.

04-06
00:02:39.182 --> 00:02:46.666
One liquid has x equal to z, but two liquids can have different
compositions whose weighted average is z.

04-07
00:02:46.846 --> 00:02:51.625
We compare states containing the same total amounts of both
components.

04-08
00:02:51.805 --> 00:02:55.581
Their pure-liquid reference contributions then cancel.

04-09
00:02:55.761 --> 00:02:59.793
In guide A two, mark the feed at zero point one two.

05-01
00:03:02.323 --> 00:03:07.934
At the marked feed, the point on the curved line represents one
homogeneous liquid.

05-02
00:03:08.114 --> 00:03:14.663
The point directly below it, on the common-tangent segment, represents
a mixture of two liquids.

05-03
00:03:14.843 --> 00:03:22.587
Those liquids have the compositions at the two contacts, with amounts
chosen to reproduce the same overall feed.

05-04
00:03:22.767 --> 00:03:25.156
Compare vertically at the same z.

05-05
00:03:25.336 --> 00:03:27.640
The split has lower energy here.

05-06
00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:34.668
Simply noticing that the homogeneous mixing energy is negative does
not establish stability.

05-07
00:03:34.848 --> 00:03:39.328
A negative value compares mixing with the pure-component reference.

05-08
00:03:39.508 --> 00:03:48.703
Our stability question compares the homogeneous liquid with other
feasible liquid states at the same overall composition.

06-01
00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:56.908
We will use the symmetric, one-parameter Margules model with A equal
to three.

06-02
00:03:57.088 --> 00:04:01.738
This is a synthetic example, not a fitted real-fluid dataset.

06-03
00:04:01.918 --> 00:04:05.396
The first two terms in g describe ideal mixing.

06-04
00:04:05.576 --> 00:04:09.117
The last term describes the excess contribution.

06-05
00:04:09.297 --> 00:04:12.561
The first derivative gives the slope of the curve.

06-06
00:04:12.741 --> 00:04:16.112
The second derivative gives its local curvature.

06-07
00:04:16.292 --> 00:04:20.772
Use natural logarithms in both the energy and the slope.

06-08
00:04:20.952 --> 00:04:28.312
At a pure endpoint, evaluate the limiting value of x times natural log
x, which is zero.

06-09
00:04:28.492 --> 00:04:33.782
Keep this model and A fixed while changing the feed in the later
comparisons.

07-01
00:04:36.312 --> 00:04:41.048
Start with local curvature at z equal to zero point one two.

07-02
00:04:41.228 --> 00:04:49.314
Take one divided by zero point one two, add one divided by zero point
eight eight, then subtract six.

07-03
00:04:49.494 --> 00:04:53.739
The result is about positive three point four six nine seven.

07-04
00:04:53.919 --> 00:05:03.860
Positive curvature means sufficiently small composition fluctuations
raise the energy, so this homogeneous state is locally stable.

07-05
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.819
It does not exclude a lower-energy split into more distant
compositions.

07-06
00:05:08.999 --> 00:05:18.215
At the middle composition, zero point five, the curvature is negative
two, so the homogeneous liquid is locally unstable.

07-07
00:05:18.395 --> 00:05:23.046
Pause for two minutes to reproduce both calculations in guide B one.

08-01
00:05:25.576 --> 00:05:28.008
Next, find the tangent contacts.

08-02
00:05:28.188 --> 00:05:32.860
The slope at each contact must equal the slope of the line joining
them.

08-03
00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:40.272
For this symmetric example, the contacts are mirror images and the
common tangent is horizontal.

08-04
00:05:40.452 --> 00:05:44.825
Solve the displayed slope equation for the low-composition root.

08-05
00:05:45.005 --> 00:05:51.874
It lies between zero point zero seven zero seven and zero point zero
seven zero eight.

08-06
00:05:52.054 --> 00:06:01.377
Refining gives approximately zero point zero seven zero seven two,
with its partner at zero point nine two nine two eight.

08-07
00:06:01.557 --> 00:06:04.181
Check the first root in guide B two.

08-08
00:06:04.361 --> 00:06:09.822
The stationary point at one half is a maximum, not a coexistence
endpoint.

09-01
00:06:12.352 --> 00:06:17.558
Now calculate amounts, using a basis of one mole of total feed.

09-02
00:06:17.738 --> 00:06:20.682
The two phase fractions must add to one.

09-03
00:06:20.862 --> 00:06:24.126
Their composition-weighted sum must equal z.

09-04
00:06:24.306 --> 00:06:28.316
Solving those two balances gives the lever rule shown here.

09-05
00:06:28.496 --> 00:06:38.502
For the beta fraction, subtract the alpha composition from the feed
composition, then divide by the difference between the two endpoint
compositions.

09-06
00:06:38.682 --> 00:06:43.034
The result is about zero point zero five seven three nine eight.

09-07
00:06:43.214 --> 00:06:49.507
The alpha fraction is one minus that, about zero point nine four two
six zero two.

09-08
00:06:49.687 --> 00:06:56.919
These are amounts of liquid phases per mole of feed, not phase
compositions and not a vapor fraction.

10-01
00:06:59.449 --> 00:07:01.966
Check the result before interpreting it.

10-02
00:07:02.146 --> 00:07:05.778
First, the alpha and beta amounts add to one mole.

10-03
00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:13.019
Next, multiply each phase amount by its component-one mole fraction
and add the contributions.

10-04
00:07:13.199 --> 00:07:18.148
The result is approximately zero point one two moles of component one.

10-05
00:07:18.328 --> 00:07:22.872
Component two then accounts for zero point eight eight moles.

10-06
00:07:23.052 --> 00:07:26.743
Small differences in the final digits come from rounding.

10-07
00:07:26.923 --> 00:07:32.214
A successful balance establishes feasibility, not minimum Gibbs
energy.

10-08
00:07:32.394 --> 00:07:40.052
If either proposed phase fraction is negative or greater than one,
those endpoints cannot represent this feed.

10-09
00:07:40.232 --> 00:07:43.475
Record the component balance in guide B three.

11-01
00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:49.610
Calculate the homogeneous energy term by term.

11-02
00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:54.228
Multiply zero point one two by its natural logarithm.

11-03
00:07:54.408 --> 00:07:59.613
This contributes about negative zero point two five four four three
two.

11-04
00:07:59.793 --> 00:08:08.049
The corresponding term for zero point eight eight contributes about
negative zero point one one two four nine three.

11-05
00:08:08.229 --> 00:08:17.232
The excess term, three times zero point one two times zero point eight
eight, is positive zero point three one six eight.

11-06
00:08:17.412 --> 00:08:24.046
Adding all three gives approximately negative zero point zero five
zero one two five.

11-07
00:08:24.226 --> 00:08:31.949
This dimensionless value is total mixing Gibbs energy divided by the
number of moles times R T.

11-08
00:08:32.129 --> 00:08:34.902
It is not yet an energy in joules.

12-01
00:08:37.432 --> 00:08:46.350
Both tangent contacts have the same dimensionless energy,
approximately negative zero point zero five eight three four one.

12-02
00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:53.356
Weighting these equal values by phase fractions that add to one gives
that same value for the split.

12-03
00:08:53.536 --> 00:08:57.547
Subtract the split energy from the homogeneous energy.

12-04
00:08:57.727 --> 00:09:03.060
The positive decrease is about zero point zero zero eight two one six.

12-05
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:11.184
The homogeneous feed is therefore metastable: locally stable, but a
feasible split lowers the total energy.

12-06
00:09:11.364 --> 00:09:21.966
For an actual total energy decrease, multiply the displayed
dimensionless decrease by the total number of moles, R, and the
absolute temperature.

12-07
00:09:22.146 --> 00:09:26.733
Pause for five minutes to complete and check guide B three and B four.

13-01
00:09:29.263 --> 00:09:33.380
This close view shows the same calculation geometrically.

13-02
00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:42.264
Locate the upper point on the homogeneous curve and the lower point on
the equilibrium segment, both at the feed composition.

13-03
00:09:42.444 --> 00:09:46.839
Their vertical separation is the dimensionless energy decrease.

13-04
00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:51.798
It is not the horizontal distance between the two phase compositions.

13-05
00:09:51.978 --> 00:09:59.898
The endpoint spacing enters the lever rule, while this vertical gap
measures the energy advantage of the feasible split.

13-06
00:10:00.078 --> 00:10:06.649
On guide B four, label the upper point homogeneous and the lower point
equilibrium split.

13-07
00:10:06.829 --> 00:10:14.765
Keep the direction of subtraction clear: homogeneous minus split is
positive when separation lowers the energy.

14-01
00:10:17.295 --> 00:10:21.818
A candidate common tangent must support the whole allowed curve.

14-02
00:10:21.998 --> 00:10:26.264
Subtract the line from g at every possible composition.

14-03
00:10:26.444 --> 00:10:28.599
No value may be negative.

14-04
00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:37.766
Equal chemical potentials identify a candidate pair, and the material
balance establishes whether its amounts are feasible.

14-05
00:10:37.946 --> 00:10:43.002
Global support completes the minimum-energy argument within this
liquid model.

14-06
00:10:43.182 --> 00:10:51.114
Checking only the two contacts is insufficient, because another part
of the curve could lie below the candidate line.

14-07
00:10:51.294 --> 00:10:54.985
A coarse plotted grid could also miss a narrow dip.

14-08
00:10:55.165 --> 00:11:02.418
Here, we can examine all stationary points and both endpoints to
verify the support condition directly.

15-01
00:11:04.948 --> 00:11:10.815
For this specific model, the stationary compositions are the two
contacts and one half.

15-02
00:11:10.995 --> 00:11:14.686
Include the pure endpoints as boundary candidates.

15-03
00:11:14.866 --> 00:11:32.572
Subtracting the horizontal common-tangent value gives approximately
positive zero point zero five eight three four one at each pure
endpoint, zero at each contact, and positive zero point one one five
one nine four at one half.

15-04
00:11:32.752 --> 00:11:34.331
None is negative.

15-05
00:11:34.511 --> 00:11:41.039
Because these cover the stationary points and boundaries, they
establish support across this curve.

15-06
00:11:41.219 --> 00:11:45.123
This is stronger than checking a few arbitrary graph points.

15-07
00:11:45.303 --> 00:11:51.383
Pause for two minutes to reproduce the middle-composition value and
complete guide B five.

16-01
00:11:53.913 --> 00:11:56.132
Now change the reference line.

16-02
00:11:56.312 --> 00:12:04.440
Tangent-plane distance, or T P D, uses the tangent to the homogeneous
curve at the feed composition.

16-03
00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:11.084
At a trial composition w, subtract that feed-tangent value from g of
w.

16-04
00:12:11.264 --> 00:12:16.640
A negative result shows that the homogeneous feed is not globally
stable.

16-05
00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:22.964
This feed tangent is different from the coexistence common tangent we
just verified.

16-06
00:12:23.144 --> 00:12:28.989
Its minimizing trial composition is not automatically a binodal
endpoint.

16-07
00:12:29.169 --> 00:12:42.954
In guide C one, keep these two tests distinct: support of the
coexistence line verifies the equilibrium construction, while feed T P
D tests the original homogeneous liquid.

17-01
00:12:45.484 --> 00:12:50.241
Open Lab zero four and go to the liquid stability experiment.

17-02
00:12:50.421 --> 00:12:59.189
Under Explore a state, choose the metastable example with A equal to
three and feed composition zero point one two.

17-03
00:12:59.369 --> 00:13:04.492
Confirm Margules, one parameter, and check both numerical inputs.

17-04
00:13:04.672 --> 00:13:06.614
Click Find equilibrium.

17-05
00:13:06.794 --> 00:13:10.399
Select the Gibbs energy and common tangent view.

17-06
00:13:10.579 --> 00:13:12.926
The graph should match our worked case.

17-07
00:13:13.106 --> 00:13:18.610
Open Verify the equilibrium to inspect the energies and component
balance.

17-08
00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:24.059
Compare the two endpoint compositions and the phase amounts with guide
B.

17-09
00:13:24.239 --> 00:13:32.410
Opening the page alone does not guarantee these settings, so selecting
and checking the preset is an essential first step.

18-01
00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:38.630
Compare the displayed results with the calculation table.

18-02
00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:48.607
The alpha liquid accounts for about ninety-four point two six percent
of the feed, and the beta liquid about five point seven four percent.

18-03
00:13:48.787 --> 00:13:54.867
The equilibrium energy is lower by about zero point zero zero eight
two one six.

18-04
00:13:55.047 --> 00:13:57.692
Now switch View to Local curvature.

18-05
00:13:57.872 --> 00:14:00.347
The feed has positive curvature.

18-06
00:14:00.527 --> 00:14:04.346
Next select Tangent-plane distance, feed stability.

18-07
00:14:04.526 --> 00:14:09.731
Its negative minimum rejects global stability of this homogeneous
feed.

18-08
00:14:09.911 --> 00:14:14.924
These observations are compatible, and together explain metastability.

18-09
00:14:15.104 --> 00:14:18.838
Allow for display rounding when checking residuals.

18-10
00:14:19.018 --> 00:14:25.439
Pause for four minutes to reproduce both views and record your
comparisons in guide C one.

19-01
00:14:27.969 --> 00:14:31.596
Predict two new cases before revealing the results.

19-02
00:14:31.776 --> 00:14:33.653
Keep A equal to three.

19-03
00:14:33.833 --> 00:14:44.218
For z equal to zero point zero two, decide whether the feed lies
between the binodal endpoints and whether the lever-rule amounts are
feasible.

19-04
00:14:44.398 --> 00:14:51.161
For z equal to one half, predict the curvature sign and the phase
amounts suggested by symmetry.

19-05
00:14:51.341 --> 00:14:54.114
Write both predictions in guide C two.

19-06
00:14:54.294 --> 00:15:01.420
Then select the corresponding Stable and Unstable presets and click
Find equilibrium for each.

19-07
00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.392
Pause for five minutes.

19-08
00:15:03.572 --> 00:15:10.896
Record your prediction, the Lab result, and a reason for any
correction before continuing to the comparison.

20-01
00:15:13.426 --> 00:15:19.208
Select the Stable preset at zero point zero two and run Find
equilibrium.

20-02
00:15:19.388 --> 00:15:22.844
The result is one liquid at the feed composition.

20-03
00:15:23.024 --> 00:15:30.426
Using the two binodal endpoints would require a negative beta amount,
so that split is infeasible.

20-04
00:15:30.606 --> 00:15:36.772
Now select the Unstable preset at zero point five and run Find
equilibrium.

20-05
00:15:36.952 --> 00:15:43.932
The homogeneous curvature is negative two, and equilibrium has equal
amounts of the two liquids.

20-06
00:15:44.112 --> 00:15:47.014
The endpoint compositions stay unchanged.

20-07
00:15:47.194 --> 00:15:49.583
Return to the comparison table.

20-08
00:15:49.763 --> 00:15:55.672
Stable, metastable, and unstable classify the initial homogeneous
feed.

20-09
00:15:55.852 --> 00:16:02.423
They must not be confused with the stable equilibrium obtained after
allowing separation.

21-01
00:16:04.953 --> 00:16:07.620
Locate the same cases on this curve.

21-02
00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:12.109
The binodal contacts bound the two-liquid equilibrium interval.

21-03
00:16:12.289 --> 00:16:21.974
The spinodals, near zero point two one one three and zero point seven
eight eight seven, mark where local curvature changes sign.

21-04
00:16:22.154 --> 00:16:28.000
Between a binodal and its neighboring spinodal, the homogeneous liquid
is metastable.

21-05
00:16:28.180 --> 00:16:31.764
Inside the spinodals it is locally unstable.

21-06
00:16:31.944 --> 00:16:36.445
Outside the binodal interval it is stable in this example.

21-07
00:16:36.625 --> 00:16:43.650
Place zero point zero two, zero point one two, and zero point five in
these regions.

21-08
00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:52.555
The equilibrium common-tangent segment applies throughout the binodal
interval, with phase amounts determined by the feed.

22-01
00:16:55.085 --> 00:16:58.477
Work the new case in guide D one on page four.

22-02
00:16:58.657 --> 00:17:03.329
Keep A equal to three and set the feed to zero point three five.

22-03
00:17:03.509 --> 00:17:06.005
First calculate local curvature.

22-04
00:17:06.185 --> 00:17:13.502
Then use the same endpoint compositions to calculate both phase
fractions and check the component balance.

22-05
00:17:13.682 --> 00:17:18.333
Calculate the homogeneous energy and its decrease on splitting.

22-06
00:17:18.513 --> 00:17:25.660
Only after your hand calculation, enter the feed in Lab zero four and
run Find equilibrium.

22-07
00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:30.789
The endpoints stay fixed because the model and conditions are
unchanged.

22-08
00:17:30.969 --> 00:17:36.409
Pause for eight minutes to attempt all five steps before watching the
worked answer.

23-01
00:17:38.939 --> 00:17:42.438
Check your calculation for zero point three five.

23-02
00:17:42.618 --> 00:17:50.554
The curvature is about negative one point six zero four four, so the
homogeneous feed is locally unstable.

23-03
00:17:50.734 --> 00:17:57.454
The beta fraction is about zero point three two five two eight nine,
and alpha is the remainder.

23-04
00:17:57.634 --> 00:18:01.922
Their weighted composition returns zero point three five.

23-05
00:18:02.102 --> 00:18:14.539
The homogeneous energy is approximately positive zero point zero three
five zero five three, and the decrease is zero point zero nine three
three nine five.

23-06
00:18:14.719 --> 00:18:20.948
Enter zero point three five in Feed mole fraction and click Find
equilibrium.

23-07
00:18:21.128 --> 00:18:25.314
Compare the same endpoints, new amounts, and energy decrease.

23-08
00:18:25.494 --> 00:18:28.460
Correct guide D one in a different color.

24-01
00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:34.083
Write a short explanation in Your explanation.

24-02
00:18:34.263 --> 00:18:43.394
State the model, A, feed composition, classification, and phase
amounts, then include one balance or energy comparison.

24-03
00:18:43.574 --> 00:18:48.395
Click Export study JSON and keep the downloaded file.

24-04
00:18:48.575 --> 00:18:53.183
To reopen it, expand Study workspace, reopen and compare cases.

24-05
00:18:53.363 --> 00:18:59.123
Choose Load studies or a comparison JSON and select your saved file.

24-06
00:18:59.303 --> 00:19:01.500
Click Restore beside the case.

24-07
00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:06.480
Check the restored inputs, then click Find equilibrium to recompute.

24-08
00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:11.832
If something differs, check the model, A, feed, selected view, and
rounding.

24-09
00:19:12.012 --> 00:19:15.809
You do not need to edit the JSON manually.

24-10
00:19:15.989 --> 00:19:20.256
Keep this file with your worksheet as evidence you can explain.

25-01
00:19:22.786 --> 00:19:26.732
Finish with guide D two, without using the Lab output.

25-02
00:19:26.912 --> 00:19:32.672
Explain why positive curvature alone does not establish global
stability.

25-03
00:19:32.852 --> 00:19:38.442
State what stays fixed and what changes when the feed moves inside
this tie line.

25-04
00:19:38.622 --> 00:19:44.680
Explain why equal chemical potentials are only part of the equilibrium
test.

25-05
00:19:44.860 --> 00:19:56.231
Finally, explain why the binodal split cannot represent a feed of zero
point zero two, even though extending the tangent line places it below
the curve there.

25-06
00:19:56.411 --> 00:19:59.291
Use one short sentence for each answer.

25-07
00:19:59.471 --> 00:20:03.076
Pause for three minutes before checking the final slide.

25-08
00:20:03.256 --> 00:20:08.952
Keep these corrections with your original worksheet; no additional
form is required.

26-01
00:20:11.482 --> 00:20:14.170
Compare your reasoning with these answers.

